\details{
\strong{Overlap.} The overlap between an episode and an alarm is the ledger time
on which the reference is the event, the row belongs to the episode, and
the detector response is positive. Intervals that only touch (one ends
where the other starts) have zero overlap.

\strong{Eligibility.} With \code{scope = "full"}, an episode is eligible when its
reference-known event time after the learning period is at least
\code{min_episode_time}. With \code{scope = "answered"}, the time counted is event
time on which the detector answered (positive or negative). Ineligible
episodes are kept in the output with the reason.

\strong{Policies.}
\itemize{
\item \code{"any_overlap"}: an episode is detected when any alarm overlaps it by more than \code{min_overlap}; an alarm is matched when it overlaps any eligible episode. One alarm may detect several episodes, and several alarms may share one episode. This is the rule used by WFDB \code{epicmp} for AF episodes (see \code{\link[=wfdb_epicmp_af]{wfdb_epicmp_af()}} for the full compatible procedure).
\item \code{"one_to_one"}: each episode is matched to at most one alarm and each alarm to at most one episode. Pairs are eligible when their overlap exceeds \code{min_overlap}. The matching maximises the number of pairs, then the total overlap; remaining ties are broken towards pairs that occur earlier in time. Because neither episodes nor alarms overlap among themselves, the candidate pairs never cross in time, and the optimum is found exactly by dynamic programming over the time-ordered pairs.
}

Alarms are classified as \code{"matched"}, \code{"redundant"} (overlaps an eligible
episode but was not paired, which happens only under \code{"one_to_one"}, e.g.
a second alarm during one episode), \code{"false"} (no overlap with any
reference event), \code{"ineligible_overlap"} (overlaps only ineligible
episodes), \code{"unassessable"} (no reference-known time) or
\code{"learning_period"}.

\strong{Latency} is reported only when the episode onset was observed, the
matched alarm has an \code{issued_at} time, and the detector was answering at
the onset. Otherwise \code{latency} is \code{NA} and \code{latency_status} says why.
}
\value{
An object of class \code{tl_match}: a list with \code{episodes} (one row per
  episode and detector), \code{alarms}, \code{pairs} and \code{settings}.
}
